package agentsdoc

import "strings"

// writeV04dTextareaSection documents the `textarea` of 0.4d for an
// authoring agent. Like the other 0.4d sections it names no path of the
// repository, since the project briefing (ProjectMarkdown) is this same
// text.
func writeV04dTextareaSection(b *strings.Builder) {
	b.WriteString("### v0.4d: `textarea`\n\n")
	b.WriteString("- `<textarea id=\"composer\" bind=\"draft\" placeholder=\"Message\" on:change=\"typed\" on:submit=\"send\"/>` is an `input` of several lines (current language only; in a document that writes `version` it is `V001`, `tag <textarea> (requires a <tui> without version)`). It takes the common attributes plus `bind`, `placeholder`, `on:change`, and `on:submit`; `secret` is `V002`. It needs an `id` (`V012`), takes no content (`V013`: its text is the value at its `bind` path), and, like an `input`, may not stand in a list `<item>` or a container `each` template (`V001`). `bind` is a path to a string; without it the runtime keeps the value by `id`. It is focusable by default (`focusable=\"false\"` turns that off) and has no focus look: `Run` puts the terminal's cursor at the caret.\n")
	b.WriteString("- **Size.** It grows from one row to its `max-height`, 8 by the built-in sheet (`textarea { width: auto; height: auto; max-height: 8; }`, `row > textarea { width: 1fr; }`), and then scrolls. Its visual lines are one `PreWrap` of the whole value at `max(1, W - 1)` columns, `W` being its content width, so `\"a\\n\"` has two lines; its intrinsic height is its visual lines, at least 1, and its intrinsic width an `input`'s, `max(width(placeholder) + 1, 20)`. It moves the layout as it grows (the nodes after it, or a modal sized by its content, move); an `input` still never does. For sizing it is not a viewport (next to a `list` it does not share the space); for scrolling it is one: the dump's `scroll` is `{\"y\": OFFSET, \"h\": EXTENT}` with the extent the larger of its content box's rows and its visual lines, the wheel moves its offset, `scrollbar: auto` paints on it, and `L006` applies.\n")
	b.WriteString("- **Value and caret.** A value the host writes (`Set`, `Batch`, `append`, a protocol `set`, the initial data) is read as run text: escape sequences removed whole, each TAB four spaces, every other control but LF removed. Everything reads that text (the painting, the visual lines, the caret, the dump, and the `value` of `on:change`, `on:submit`, and a keymap row) while the store keeps what the host wrote until the next edit that changes the text, which writes the normalized text with the edit applied. The caret starts at the end of the value, and goes to the end of a value changed from outside (not by the textarea's own edits); a write of the value the store already holds is no change. A click and the wheel do not move it. There is no undo. `up`, `down`, `pgup`, and `pgdn` keep a goal column that every other placement of the caret sets. The offset follows the caret by the least change after an edit or a caret move, and shows the caret on a first frame.\n")
	b.WriteString("- **Keys** (a focused, enabled textarea; every other key goes on to the keymap). Every printable key and `space` types; `backspace` and `ctrl+h` delete the cluster before the caret, `delete` the one after it (a line break included); `ctrl+u` deletes to the start of the hard line, `ctrl+k` to its end (at the end of a hard line that is not the last it deletes the line break); `ctrl+w` and `alt+backspace` delete a word backward, within the hard line, or the line break at its start; `left` and `right` cross line breaks, `home`/`ctrl+a` and `end`/`ctrl+e` go to the start and end of the hard line; `alt+b`/`alt+f`, and `left`/`right` held with `alt` or `ctrl`, move by words across lines; `up` and `down` move one visual line and are not consumed on the first and last line, so `<bind keys=\"up\" action=\"history_prev\" when=\"#composer:focus\"/>` fires from the first line only; `pgup` and `pgdn` move by the content box's rows and are consumed only while the textarea scrolls. `enter` fires `on:submit` when it has one (the text is unchanged: the host clears it) and inserts a line break otherwise; `shift+enter`, `alt+enter`, and the LF byte (`ctrl+j`) always insert one. `tab`, `shift+tab`, `esc`, `ctrl+enter`, `ctrl+c`, the F keys, and the other `ctrl+X` and `alt+X` are not consumed. Because it takes every printable key, a keymap row for one (`y`) never fires while an enabled textarea has focus: disable the textarea (`disabled=\"path\"`, which keeps its value) or move the focus while such a row must act.\n")
	b.WriteString("- **Paste and events.** A bracketed paste keeps its line breaks (CR LF and a lone CR become LF), is normalized as run text, and is one edit with one `on:change`. There is no heuristic for a terminal without bracketed paste: its CRs are `enter` keys. `on:change` and `on:submit` carry `{\"source\": ID, \"keys\": {}, \"value\": TEXT}`, and so does a keymap row fired while it has focus. `move-*` and `check-*` never apply to it: with `to=` or a `when` that is exactly `#id:focus` naming one they are `B007`.\n")
	b.WriteString("- **Tools.** The dump's node has `text`, the value line breaks included (never the placeholder; omitted when empty), `scroll`, and, on the focused textarea only, `caret`: `{\"line\", \"col\", \"x\", \"y\"}`, the caret's hard line and its column in it, from 0, in columns, then the cell where `Run` puts the cursor; `x` and `y` are left out when the caret's visual line is outside the content box, where `Run` hides the cursor. A focused `textarea` and `<hints/>`: the hint items for `up`, `down`, `pgup`, and `pgdn` are chosen again after layout, from the frame being built alone, so a `dump` of a fresh app (whose caret is at the end of the value) shows the same items as a session at that caret. `tuimark ir` shows the kind `textarea`, with `bind` in `bind` and `placeholder` in `attrs`. `inspect` explains it as any node. `fmt` writes it as a self-closing leaf.\n\n")
}
